@nellynelson965

Sorry to jump in, but let me be allowed to express the complete opposite view: cost per frame is a f-in useless metrics. Are you are such a nerd who cannot even use the calculator to divide one number with another and repeat it as many times as needed? Since not only the price of the card can change, based on geographical location, or with the passage of the time or with different models (the MSRP is only applicable in the US, and for example here in the Netherlands the cheapest 7800XTs are 2-fan models starting from 560 EUR, but the better 3-fan models start from 585 EUR, so which price would you recommend for me to calculate with?), but to calculate with the GPU price only can also be debated. If you are upgrading your GPU only, then it may be useful, but what if you are building a new system? Or replacing core components, motherboard, CPU as well to be to able push higher FPS? Then calculating with the total system / upgrade cost is more appropriate, in my opinion.
On the other hand, power consumption does not change, and if you are so lucky that the electricity bill does not matter for you, you should be glad to enjoy it. You think that only poor people on tight budget can be interested in this aspect, but why are you so certain about it? Should I remind you that generally newer technologies are more power efficient but also more expensive the previous ones, therefore usually richer people buy that new technology first? It seems that you are very bad at mathematics, so can you please take a glance on the table again at 9:29? The difference between a 7700XT and 4060Ti by 4 hrs / day at 20 cent/KWh is 27.45. How much is that per month? =2.29$ Would you notice the increase in the power bill? I bet you won't. So the monthly impact is quite small, but the small numbers add up in the long run. Therefore to present these figures can be beneficial for people to understand and visualise the financial effect of different power consumptions.
